---
# CAN THE PROJECTION RATE STILL BE EXPLAINED? -- the question the read-model panels could not
# answer for a whole evening, because half the answer had no metric.
#
# `ReadModelProjector` is fed by TWO change-stream cursors and only one of them was ever counted:
#
#   kinowo_worker_movie_change_events_total{country,op}       the `movies` cursor
#   kinowo_worker_screenings_change_events_total{country,op}  the `screenings` cursor -- one event
#       per changed screenings DOCUMENT, i.e. per (film, cinema slot), and normally the LARGER of
#       the two by an order of magnitude
#
# Each event costs one `kinowo_worker_readmodel_project_calls_total`, so in a healthy worker the
# projection rate tracks the SUM of the two within noise. On 2026-09-04 worker-de logged 184
# movies events against 10,114 projections in 85 minutes and every panel that could have named the
# cause plotted only the movies line -- which was flat. The number was not wrong, it was
# unattributable, and there is no threshold that catches "unattributable". A RATIO against the
# metered input is the only thing that does.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT ALERT ON.
#
#   The redundant-write share. `kinowo_worker_screenings_writes_total{outcome="unchanged"}` counts
#   writes the store recognised as no-ops and dropped. A HIGH share there is the guard EARNING ITS
#   KEEP, not a fault -- `replaceFilm` is film-wide while its callers' change is one venue, so a
#   wide release legitimately offers hundreds of unchanged rows per scrape (297 of 298 on prod DE).
#   Alerting on it would page for healthy operation, which is how a channel gets ignored. It is a
#   panel, and the number to read once this rule has already told you something is wrong.
#
#   Projections with no read-model output. `readmodel_writes_total` sits near zero even when
#   projection is hot -- that is what the metadata-reuse cache is FOR -- so "hot with no output" is
#   the normal shape, not the broken one.
#
# THE `country` LABEL comes from the SCRAPE (scrape-kinowo-apps.yaml labels each worker's NodePort
# target), not from the application, so it is true by construction.
groups:
  - name: read-model-projection
    rules:
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE PROJECTOR IS RUNNING FAR AHEAD OF ANYTHING THAT COULD BE TRIGGERING IT.
      #
      # 3x, not 1.1x. One event is one projection by construction, but the two rates are measured
      # over the same window from different counters and a burst that straddles the window edge
      # skews the ratio either way; a cursor that reopens and replays from its resume token counts
      # its events again and legitimately runs the ratio under 1. Three times the metered input is
      # far outside any of that, and the shape this exists for ran at FIFTY-FIVE times.
      #
      # THE 0.5/s FLOOR IS THE POINT OF THE RULE, not decoration. At PL's quiet-hour 0.03/s a
      # single stray projection against zero events is an infinite ratio, and every country idles
      # there overnight. The floor is what keeps this rule about volume that costs something: 0.5
      # projections/s is already above every country's measured baseline over 2026-09-01..04
      # (PL 0.1-0.6, DE 0.02-0.4, US 0.6-1.4).
      #
      # A MISSING screenings COUNTER MAKES THIS SILENT, not loud: the sum of two instant vectors is
      # empty when either side has no series, so this rule says nothing until a build that exports
      # both is deployed. That is the safe direction, and `ReadModelProjectionMetricsAbsent` below
      # is what notices the metrics have gone entirely.
      - alert: ReadModelProjectionTriggerUnaccounted
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_readmodel_project_calls_total[30m]))
            > 3 * (
              sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_movie_change_events_total[30m]))
              + sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_screenings_change_events_total[30m]))
            )
          ) > 0.5
        # ONE HOUR. The rate windows are already 30m, so this is not waiting for the condition to
        # be real -- it is waiting out a worker rollout, where a restarted cursor replays from its
        # resume token and both counters restart at zero at slightly different moments. The fleet
        # redeployed five times in the four hours before this rule was written.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} is projecting {{ $value | printf \"%.2f\" }}/s more than its change streams can explain"
          description: >-
            `readmodel_project_calls_total` is running more than 3x the combined rate of the two
            cursors that are supposed to drive it, so something is projecting rows that no metered
            change-stream event asked for. Panel 47 on the application dashboard plots all three
            series together and panel 48 draws the sum. Check, in order: whether a THIRD trigger
            has been added and not metered (that is what this rule exists to catch -- the
            `screenings` cursor itself was unmetered until 2026-09-05 and cost an evening);
            whether `ReadModelProjector.reconcile` is being called by something other than a
            backfill script (the scheduled sweep re-projects nothing and logs "reprojected 0
            doc(s)", so its log line settles that in one grep); and whether a cursor is reopening
            in a loop, which replays its resume token and inflates projections without inflating
            the event counters proportionally.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE COMPANION, in the shape mongodb.rules and cinema-scrape.rules use throughout: a rule
      # that can only fire on a series has to be paired with one that fires on the series'
      # ABSENCE, or the monitoring's own failure looks exactly like health. The rule above is
      # silent when its inputs are missing, and silence is also what a well-behaved projector
      # looks like.
      #
      # `absent()` OVER THE WHOLE METRIC, not per country: a per-country form would page for four
      # countries throughout any deliberate single-country stop (the 2026-08-02 poland-only month).
      - alert: ReadModelProjectionMetricsAbsent
        expr: absent(kinowo_worker_readmodel_project_calls_total)
        # ONE HOUR: longer than a rollout of every worker in the fleet.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "No worker is publishing read-model projection metrics"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_readmodel_project_calls_total` has no series from any country, so
            ReadModelProjectionTriggerUnaccounted cannot fire and the read model's whole input
            path is unwatched. Either every worker is down -- in which case TargetDown in
            monitoring-self.rules is also firing and is the better alert to read -- or the
            projector is no longer wired to `WorkerTaskMetrics`, which TargetDown would NOT catch
            because the pod is up and serving /metrics without this family in it.
